# Whether anything wants the AWDL radio. Written by barqsharingd, read by barqd.
#
# A property rather than binder: barqd holds CAP_NET_ADMIN and CAP_NET_RAW, and a
# property read is a shared-memory load of one boolean -- no parser, no IPC surface
# in the privileged process. See barqd's WANT_PROP comment for why this is also not
# ctl.start/ctl.stop.
# Prefix, not one exact key: the namespace now carries the Wi-Fi frequency as well
# as the wanted flag, and an exact rule for one leaves the other on default_prop --
# where set_prop on our own type is still denied, which fails silently.
barq.awdl.          u:object_r:barq_awdl_prop:s0

# The advertised device name. EXACT KEY, not the `persist.barq.` prefix.
#
# The prefix argument above is right for `barq.awdl.`, where one process owns every key
# in the namespace. It is wrong here: `persist.barq.` is SHARED. barqd reads
# persist.barq.mosey_config, .channels, .sta_freq and .country as operator overrides,
# and labelling the prefix pulled all four onto a type barqd has no grant for -- so
# every one of them silently stopped working, including the regulatory country
# override, while the log filled with barqd read denials.
#
# One key needs a label because one process must SET it. The rest are read-only knobs
# that were working on the default label; leave them there.
persist.barq.name   u:object_r:barq_name_prop:s0
